==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{term|gostly|lang=enm}}, {{term|gastlich|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{m|ang|gāstlīċ||spiritual, holy, clerical (not lay), ghastly, ghostly, spectral}}, equivalent to {{suffix|ghost|ly|lang=en}}. Cognate with {{etyl|sco|-}} {{term|gostly|lang=sco}}, {{term|gastly|lang=sco}}, {{term|gaistlie||spiritual, ghastly, terrifying|lang=sco}}, {{etyl|fy|-}} {{term|geastlik||spiritual, clerical, religious|lang=fy}}, {{etyl|nl|-}} {{term|geestelijk||spiritual, clerical, ecclesiastical|lang=nl}}, {{etyl|de|-}} {{term|geistlich||spiritual, sacred, religious|lang=de}}, {{etyl|da|-}} {{term|geistlig||ecclesiastical, clerical|lang=da}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en-us-ghostly.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|er}}

# Of or pertaining to [[ghost]]s or [[spirit]]s.
#: ''a '''ghostly''' figure with a hood.''
#: ''the graveyard was haunted by a '''ghostly''' figure of a young girl.''
#: ''the '''ghostly''' moaning was heard from upstairs.''
# [[spooky|Spooky]]; [[frightening]].
# Relating to the soul; not carnal or secular; spiritual.
#: ''a '''ghostly''' confessor''
#* Book of Common Prayer
#*: Save and defend us from our '''ghostly''' enemies.
#* Jeremy Taylor
#*: One of the '''ghostly''' children of St. Jerome.

